<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Books and Movies – Lesson # 1</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Liked Your Presentation Because...</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow audio narratives. Receptive: Show recognition of specific details when listening to explanations.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives by responding to questions in discussions about movies and books, showing comprehension of specific preferences (e.g., "Why does Jose like this book?" "Because it has amazing characters").<br>Can identify key points shared by classmates when listening to explanations or participating in discussions (e.g., "Who likes action movies?" "Juan").<br>Can answer simple questions about favorite genres after listening to peers (e.g., "What type of book does Maria like?" "Mysteries").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing pictures of popular movies and book covers from different genres (e.g., action, mystery, comedy, documentary, poetry). Students raise their hands to indicate if they like the movie or book genre. The teacher asks simple questions like, "Who likes action movies?" and "What kind of books do you enjoy reading?" to activate prior knowledge about preferences.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher uses visuals (posters, slides) of different movie/book genres.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages students to give one-word answers or short phrases.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the target vocabulary (movie, book, poetry, character, plot, theme, documentary, genre, enjoy, like, prefer, entertaining, engaging, emotional). The teacher explicitly models how to express preferences using "I like reading fiction books because they are exciting" and "I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling." The teacher also models the pronunciation of /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ in verbs like "likes," "enjoys," and "watches" and contrastive vowel sounds like /ʊ/ in "full" and /uː/ in "fool" using example words like "book" and "movie."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I like watching documentaries,' am I talking about a movie or a book?" (Expected answer: A movie.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a story has good people and bad people, are those the characters or the plot?" (Expected answer: The characters.)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Books and Movies".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio narrative (approx. 1 minute) of two teenagers, Jose and Ana, discussing their favorite books and movies. The dialogue incorporates the target vocabulary (movie, book, character, plot, genre, enjoy, like, prefer, entertaining, engaging) and grammar (present simple for preferences, verb + -ing + noun + because, comparing preferences).</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Audio content example:</b><br>Jose: "Hey Ana, did you read 'The Secret of the Old House'? It's an amazing mystery book!"<br>Ana: "Oh, I haven't. I usually prefer watching movies over reading books because they are more entertaining. Yesterday, I watched a comedy; it was about two funny characters trying to solve a puzzle. The plot was so engaging!"<br>Jose: "I like reading mystery books because the characters are always clever and the plot keeps me guessing. I also enjoy watching documentaries sometimes, but I definitely prefer books."<br>Ana: "That's interesting! I think I like action movies more than comedies because they are more thrilling. But I understand why you enjoy books."</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will follow audio narratives and show recognition of specific details when listening to explanations of preferences.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen once and answer: "What are Jose and Ana talking about?" (Expected: Their favorite books and movies/what they like).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Specific details - True/False):</b> Students listen again and answer True or False to statements like:<br>a) Jose prefers watching movies over reading books. (False)<br>b) Ana watched a comedy movie yesterday. (True)<br>c) Jose likes mystery books because of the characters and plot. (True)<br>d) Ana prefers comedies over action movies. (False)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for preferences, reasons, and comparative statements using present simple, verb + -ing + noun + because, and comparative preferences. Reinforce vocabulary (movie, book, character, plot, genre, enjoy, like, prefer, entertaining, engaging, emotional) and pronunciation of verb endings (/s/, /z/, /ɪz/) and vowel sounds (/ʊ/, /uː/).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening for Gaps):</b> The teacher plays the audio narrative again, this time with a short transcript handout where 5-7 key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>genre, engaging, plot, prefer, entertaining</i>) and specific reasons are missing. Students listen and fill in the blanks. The teacher pauses the audio after each missing word to allow students to write.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Identifying Preferences):</b> The teacher plays 4-5 short, individual audio clips (10-15 seconds each). Each clip features a different person describing a book or movie they like or prefer (e.g., "I enjoy reading poetry because it's emotional," "He likes watching documentaries, but he prefers action movies"). After each clip, students choose the correct preference from two options (e.g., "Speaker 1 likes: a) poetry b) novels" or "Speaker 2 prefers: a) documentaries b) action movies"). This activity also helps students focus on differentiating words like "full" and "fool" if those words are included in the audio to practice /ʊ/ and /uː/.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listening & Confirming):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays another set of 3 short audio descriptions of different books/movies (e.g., a student describing a book's character, another describing a movie's theme). After each description, students individually write down one key detail they heard (e.g., "The character is brave," "The theme is friendship"). Then, they turn to their partner and compare their answers, saying "I heard that the character is brave. What did you hear?" to confirm their listening comprehension.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Books and Movies".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Listening for Feedback." Students will listen to short (30-45 seconds) pre-recorded audio descriptions (or live peer descriptions) of a favorite book or movie, prepared by a few classmates or the teacher. After listening to each description, students will provide written feedback, explicitly stating what they liked about the *presentation* using the target phrase "I liked your presentation because..." and integrating the specific details they heard and target vocabulary.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays 3-4 different audio clips, one at a time. Each clip features a student (or simulated voice) briefly describing their favorite book or movie, explaining why they like it, mentioning the genre, characters, plot, or theme.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each audio clip, students are given 1-2 minutes to write down their feedback using a sentence frame: "I liked your presentation because the ______ (e.g., character / plot / theme / description) was very ______ (e.g., engaging / emotional / entertaining)." or "I enjoyed listening because you clearly expressed why you prefer ______."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice listening carefully for specific details and reasons to formulate their feedback. The focus is on demonstrating comprehension of the spoken input.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher collects the written feedback at the end of the activity.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Observation checklist focusing on students' ability to identify specific details and express comprehension in writing.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will complete the "Listening for Feedback" task from Stage 4. Their written feedback for each audio description will serve as the assessment artifact. Teacher will review their written responses.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension of details:</b> Did the student correctly identify specific details (e.g., genre, character, plot, theme) from the audio?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Did the student integrate at least two target vocabulary words (e.g., engaging, entertaining, emotional, character, plot) in their feedback?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of feedback:</b> Is the feedback sentence ("I liked your presentation because...") clear and grammatically comprehensible?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you learned today about books and movies?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How easy or difficult was it to understand what your classmates (or the audio speakers) liked and why?" (Easy / Okay / Difficult)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to discussions about books and movies was most challenging for you today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can listening to others describe their preferences help you understand them better?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., Student engagement with audio, clarity of instructions, effectiveness of CCQs).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next listening lesson? (e.g., Slower audio pace, more visual support, additional vocabulary pre-teaching, focusing on specific phonemes if students struggled with /ʊ/ and /uː/).</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will read a short text or review about a book or movie. This will reuse the vocabulary introduced today and further explore genres and preferences, building on their listening comprehension by seeing the words in written form.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>